import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'/home/claude/work'); from gen import *
W='/tmp/tlv-src/apps/web'; T='https://thefirstbeast.com'
H=f'{T}/the-harlot-and-the-scarlet-beast'
make(16,f"This is a summary. The Fathers on the seven bowls are set out more fully on The First Beast, the sister site on Revelation 13: [The Three Unclean Spirits]({T}/the-three-unclean-spirits) for 16:12–16, and [The Harlot and the Scarlet Beast]({H}) for the seventh bowl.",
[('/topics/armageddon','Armageddon'),(f'{T}/the-three-unclean-spirits','The Three Unclean Spirits (The First Beast)')],
[('16:1–9','The first four bowls',[
 ('Literal or spiritual?','Oecumenius allows either a plague at the end or a figure of the distress the Lord foretold (Matthew 24:6–8). St. Caesarius of Arles reads the plagues as spiritual: the mortal sins of the proud are “sores in their souls.” St. Andrew of Caesarea adds a note of mercy: scourges in this life are meant to lessen what would follow, and he prays that we be corrected “in a fatherly manner.”'),
 ('The scorching sun.','Primasius does not read the fire of Gehenna here, since no blasphemy is possible there. It is a present torment: what gives light to the elect gives fire to the wicked. Blasphemers, he says, have the habit of blaming God and not themselves.'),
]),
('16:10–11','The throne of the beast',[
 ('Darkness.','Oecumenius: the end of the Antichrist’s tyranny at the Lord’s coming (2 Thessalonians 2:8). Andrew: his kingdom is left without the light of the Sun of righteousness. Primasius and St. Bede read a darkness already present: the beast’s followers are darkened by earthly happiness and rejoice where they should repent.'),
]),
('16:12–16','The three unclean spirits',[
 ('Why frogs?','Oecumenius and Andrew: demons, who love the mud of a loose life. Primasius, following Tyconius: false teachers, who like frogs at night make “a great noise” and keep the unlearned from the truth. Caesarius: hypocrites, who seem to live in the water of Baptism and live in what others laid aside there.'),
 ('The gathering of the kings.','Tyconius does not expect all the nations to assemble in one place. Each nation, captive to its own superstitions, will wish to serve in that army.'),
 ('“Blessed is the one who keeps his clothes ready.”','Tyconius: the garment of Baptism (Galatians 3:27). Andrew: good works, and he prays that the Lord wash the robes of our souls. None of the Fathers cited names the spirits, and neither does this site.'),
]),
('16:17–21','The seventh bowl',[
 ('“It is done.”','Andrew: the commandment of God accomplished. The earthquake is the change of all that exists (Hebrews 12:26). Bede: the end, when the last enemy is destroyed (1 Corinthians 15:26).'),
 ('The great city in three parts.','Caesarius: every people under heaven, divided into the pagans, the heretics and false Christians, and the catholic Church. Andrew: true Christians are now mingled with those who have the name alone, and “the cleansing fire of temptations” will unmask them.'),
 ('Islands and mountains.','Primasius: the Church, which withdraws from the ruin. Andrew: the churches and their leaders, who will prefer the mountains and caves to the cities for the sake of their faith.'),
]),
],f'{W}/src/data/fathers/chapter-16.tsx'); wire(16,W)
make(17,f"This is a summary. The Fathers on chapter 17 are set out in full on The First Beast: [The Harlot and the Scarlet Beast]({H}).",
[('/topics/babylon','Babylon'),(H,'The Harlot and the Scarlet Beast (The First Beast)')],
[('17:1–6','Who is the harlot?',[
 ('Rome.','St. Victorinus and Oecumenius read the city that persecuted the saints in their own centuries.'),
 ('Earthly rule as a whole.','St. Andrew of Caesarea knows that reading and looks wider: Scripture gives such names to cities for their conduct, and old Rome lost its dominion long ago.'),
 ('The multitude of the lost.','Primasius and St. Bede read a people, those who have given themselves over to demons, set against the company of believers betrothed to one husband.'),
 ('The gold cup.','Tyconius: the gold is hypocrisy, fair outside and full of uncleanness within.'),
]),
('17:7–8','“It was, and is not, and is to come”',[
 ('The devil before and after the Cross.','Andrew and Bede: he was powerful before the Cross; by it he lost his hold on the nations; at the end he will come again through the Antichrist, and go to destruction.'),
 ('A people that renews itself.','Primasius and St. Caesarius of Arles: the body of the wicked passes away and is replaced, one generation copying the last.'),
]),
('17:9–14','The seven kings and the ten',[
 ('Five readings.','Victorinus: the emperors of John’s own day. Oecumenius: the seven emperors who persecuted the Church most. Andrew: seven kingdoms that have ruled the world in turn. St. Hippolytus: seven ages of the world. Primasius and Bede: no list at all; seven is the fullness of earthly rule. So many readings show how little the Church has settled.'),
 ('The ten kings.','Oecumenius and Andrew expect ten kings at the end, as Daniel foresaw (Daniel 7:24), whose reign is brief.'),
]),
('17:15–18','The kings turn on the harlot',[
 ('Enemy and avenger.','Andrew finds it remarkable that the devil is both: through the ten horns he opposes Christ, and through them he also lays waste the city that left God’s laws for its pleasures.'),
 ('Two cities.','Bede: there are two cities in the world, one that rises from the abyss and one that comes down from heaven.'),
]),
],f'{W}/src/data/fathers/chapter-17.tsx'); wire(17,W)
make(18,f"This is a summary. The Fathers on the fall of Babylon are set out in full on The First Beast, under “The commentators on her fall”: [The Harlot and the Scarlet Beast]({H}).",
[('/topics/babylon','Babylon'),(H,'The Harlot and the Scarlet Beast (The First Beast)')],
[('18:1–3','“Fallen, fallen is Babylon”',[
 ('No city of stones.','St. Caesarius of Arles asks whether the ruins of one city could hold every unclean spirit. The city that holds them all is the city of the devil, spread through the whole earth in wicked people. Babylon means “confusion”; Jerusalem, “vision of peace.”'),
 ('Fallen already.','Primasius, following Tyconius: by collapsing under the weight of her sins she showed herself fit for demons.'),
 ('The merchants.','Primasius: those who “trade their souls” for temporal abundance (Matthew 16:26).'),
]),
('18:4–5','“Come out of her, my people”',[
 ('A separation of the will.','Primasius: until the end of the world the good allow the wicked to be mingled with them. The righteous go out less by a separation of body than by the difference of a sound will (2 Timothy 2:19).'),
 ('Babylon divided.','Caesarius: one comes out of Babylon whenever one abandons an evil way of life. Citizens of both cities are found “in each house and in the one church and in each city.”'),
 ('St. Jerome to a friend in Rome.','Writing from Bethlehem, he quotes this verse to urge a friend to the monastic life, and grants at once that Rome has a holy church, the trophies of apostles and martyrs and a true confession of Christ (*Letter* 46).'),
]),
('18:6–19','“Repay her double”; the laments',[
 ('By the sword of the word.','St. Augustine: the vengeance is done daily by speaking. The friend who begins to blush at having worshiped what he made “is about to die that he may live” (*Explanation of Psalm 149*).'),
 ('Less than she deserves.','Oecumenius: God does not take vengeance twice, and punishes far less than is deserved.'),
 ('They weep for themselves.','Caesarius: the kings mourn their lost prosperity, not their sin. Primasius reads more hopefully: to stand far off and cry out can be the turning of a soul, a “fruitful penance.”'),
]),
('18:20–24','The millstone',[
 ('“Rejoice over her.”','St. Andrew of Caesarea: the apostles and prophets rejoice at the end of sin, not at anyone’s hardship.'),
 ('The turning of time.','Caesarius: the ages grind down those who love the world. This is the city Cain founded on his brother’s blood (Genesis 4:17).'),
 ('Two stones.','St. Bede: the world is an unstable millstone, sunk by the weight of its sins. The Church is the firm rock that withstands the storm (Matthew 7:24–25).'),
]),
],f'{W}/src/data/fathers/chapter-18.tsx'); wire(18,W)
